Z||000000||MR2KLR||11-29-2001||07:44 AM||||<BR>Can I just splice in my new pump? I got my old tank out last night and could not find the plug to un hook it, so I cut it off as close as I could to the old pump. I'm wondering if the tank slides around down there, if a thick splice would rub through and short. will it be OK?<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.tripod.com/wawoonigootphet/images/af/3in1_vu1.jpg">  <A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT-maniac||11-29-2001||03:09 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by MR2KLR:<BR>[B]<BR>Can I just splice in my new pump? I got my old tank out last night and could not find the plug to un hook it, so I cut it off as close as I could to the old pump. I'm wondering if the tank slides around down there, if a thick splice would rub through and short. will it be OK?<P>Well the plug to unhook the fuel pump harness is attached with a plastic clip to the firewall just below where the main wire bundles go through to the interior of the car. It's a real pain to get apart and get back in again but since you've now cut the harness I guess it doesn't matter any more.<BR>I wouldn't worry about the tank moving down there, it's wedged in pretty good and I doubt it will move at all once you put it back up.<BR>One thing I would be carefull of though is making sure that the new connection you'll have to make to the wiring harness is water tight. Just buy some heat shrink tubing and connect the wires with either quick disconnect terminals or solderless connectors. Just make sure the connection is sealed and water tight with the heat shrink.<BR>I've done two fuel pumps now and it's not too bad of a job. <P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero005a.jpg"> <P>85 WS6 GT partly modified<BR>87 GT Loaded resto project||||reg||1
